<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
    <!--
      Logitech Brio 100 under the rear of the chassis, aimed at the drill.

      A VIEW CAMERA, AND ONLY THAT. Every other camera on this robot is a
      RealSense D435i feeding a perception pipeline; this one is a UVC webcam
      with no depth sensor and nothing subscribes to it but the operator's
      screen. That distinction runs through the whole stack: the driver is
      usb_cam rather than realsense2_camera, and the downlink carries colour
      only - see camera_downlink.py's colour-only mode. Do not point a planner,
      an octomap or a grasp node at it. There is no depth to read and the
      intrinsics below are a catalogue figure, not a calibration.

      WHERE IT SITS. base_link, +X forward, +Y left. The belly plate at the
      rear of base.stl is a flat down-facing surface at z = 0.000, spanning
      x -0.251..-0.151 and y -0.215..+0.213, and the camera bolts under it.

      IT CANNOT GO ON THE CENTRELINE. The drill assembly occupies a continuous
      channel y -0.090..+0.090 from x = -0.195 all the way back to x = -0.635,
      from z = -0.022 upward - so the middle of that plate is not free surface,
      it is the drill. That leaves two clear strips, y +0.090..+0.213 and
      y -0.215..-0.090, each about 123 mm wide. The camera body is ~94 mm
      across, so it fits in either with a little over 10 mm a side. +Y is used;
      rear_camera_y is the one-line change to move it to the other side.

      WHAT IT LOOKS AT. The optical axis is aimed at the auger axis where it
      meets the ground: x = -0.5095 (drill_bit_joint), y = 0, z = -0.2064
      (the ground plane, base_link_height below base_link). That is 0.367 m
      away, and the angles below are computed from those two points rather than
      rounded to something tidy - change either end and both must be recomputed.

          yaw   -2.641731 rad   (-151.36 deg)
          pitch  0.549152 rad   ( 31.46 deg)

      The sight line was ray-traced against base.stl and drill_body.stl: it
      passes clear of both, under the drill body, which is the whole reason the
      camera is mounted low rather than on the rear panel. What it therefore
      sees is the auger below z = -0.022 - the section standing above the hole
      and entering it - plus the ground around the bit. The mast, the feed
      carriage and the sample container are ABOVE the drill body's underside
      and are not in this view; there is no camera on them.

      A 0.265 x 0.199 m window at that range, from the FOV below. The auger is
      50 mm across, so it stands about a fifth of the frame wide.
    -->

    <!-- Mount point: the camera BODY CENTRE, not a bolt face. The bracket
         between the belly plate and the camera is not modelled - there is no
         CAD for it - so this is where the camera is, not how it is held. -->
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_x" value="-0.235"/>
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_y" value="0.150"/>
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_z" value="-0.015"/>

    <!-- Aim, computed from the mount point to the bit/ground point above. -->
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_yaw"   value="-2.641731"/>
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_pitch" value="0.549152"/>

    <!-- Brio 100 body, in CAMERA axes (+X out of the lens, +Y left, +Z up):
         depth x width x height. Product-page figures, not measured off the
         part - the first thing to correct when it is in hand. Width is the one
         that matters, because it is what the fit between the drill body and
         the edge of the belly plate is spent on. -->
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_depth"  value="0.026"/>
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_width"  value="0.094"/>
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_height" value="0.028"/>
    <!-- 75 g, catalogue. Taken OUT of base_link's aggregate in aries_base.xacro
         rather than added on top, so the rover still totals 90 kg - the same
         bookkeeping the drill and stack light links use. -->
    <xacro:property name="rear_camera_mass" value="0.075"/>

    <material name="rear_camera_body"><color rgba="0.12 0.12 0.13 1.0"/></material>

    <link name="rear_camera_link">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <box size="${rear_camera_depth} ${rear_camera_width} ${rear_camera_height}"/>
            </geometry>
            <material name="rear_camera_body"/>
        </visual>
        <!-- Collision is present and is the real body. It hangs 177 mm above
             the ground under the tail of the rover, which is exactly where a
             rock catches on a slope. It is well outside the arm's reach and is
             not disabled against anything in the SRDF. -->
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <box size="${rear_camera_depth} ${rear_camera_width} ${rear_camera_height}"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
        <!-- Uniform box of the collision dimensions, m/12*(a^2+b^2) - the same
             derivation the two D435i links use. -->
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="${rear_camera_mass}"/>
            <inertia
                ixx="${rear_camera_mass * (rear_camera_width * rear_camera_width + rear_camera_height * rear_camera_height) / 12}"
                ixy="0" ixz="0"
                iyy="${rear_camera_mass * (rear_camera_depth * rear_camera_depth + rear_camera_height * rear_camera_height) / 12}"
                iyz="0"
                izz="${rear_camera_mass * (rear_camera_depth * rear_camera_depth + rear_camera_width * rear_camera_width) / 12}"/>
        </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="rear_camera_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="base_link"/>
        <child link="rear_camera_link"/>
        <origin xyz="${rear_camera_x} ${rear_camera_y} ${rear_camera_z}"
                rpy="0 ${rear_camera_pitch} ${rear_camera_yaw}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ROS optical convention: +Z out of the lens, +X right, +Y down. This is
         the frame the image is expressed in and the one CameraInfo refers to;
         rear_camera_link is the body frame, +X out of the lens. Same -pi/2, 0,
         -pi/2 pair the D435i frames use. -->
    <joint name="rear_camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="rear_camera_link"/>
        <child link="rear_camera_optical_frame"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 ${-pi/2}"/>
    </joint>
    <link name="rear_camera_optical_frame"/>

    <!--
      Simulation. One colour sensor, no depth: the hardware has none, and a
      depth_camera here would put a stream in simulation that the real rover
      can never publish.

      FOV IS A CATALOGUE FIGURE. Logitech quote 58 deg diagonal for the Brio
      100 at its native 16:9. Captured at 4:3 the sensor is cropped
      horizontally, keeping the vertical angle, which works out at 39.8 deg
      horizontal / 30.4 deg vertical - 0.695 rad below. It has NOT been
      checked against the camera. Calibrate it before anyone measures anything
      off this image; for a view stream it is close enough to frame the shot.

      15 Hz, matching the other two sim sensors. Every render sensor competes
      for GPU time and the rover world already runs at RTF ~0.54.
    -->
    <gazebo reference="rear_camera_link">
        <self_collide>0</self_collide>
        <enable_wind>0</enable_wind>
        <kinematic>0</kinematic>
        <sensor name="rear_camera_color" type="camera">
            <camera name="rear_camera">
                <horizontal_fov>0.695</horizontal_fov>
                <image>
                    <width>640</width>
                    <height>480</height>
                    <format>RGB_INT8</format>
                </image>
                <clip>
                    <near>0.05</near>
                    <far>50</far>
                </clip>
                <noise>
                    <type>gaussian</type>
                    <mean>0.0</mean>
                    <stddev>0.007</stddev>
                </noise>
            </camera>
            <always_on>1</always_on>
            <update_rate>15</update_rate>
            <visualize>false</visualize>
            <!-- image_raw, not color/image_raw: usb_cam names its topics
                 <camera>/image_raw on hardware and the two must agree, or the
                 downlink reads a topic that only exists in one of them. -->
            <topic>rear_camera/image_raw</topic>
            <gz_frame_id>rear_camera_optical_frame</gz_frame_id>
        </sensor>
    </gazebo>

    <!--
      Keep this link out of base_link's lump, for the reason stacklight.xacro
      documents at length: sdformat numbers lumped visuals in sequence, and the
      stack light's tiers are addressed BY THAT NAME at runtime
      (aries_bringup/nodes/stacklight_gz_visual.py). Lumping one more visual
      into the chassis renumbers them and the light quietly stops lighting.
      A gz sensor also needs a link of its own to hang on.
    -->
    <gazebo reference="rear_camera_joint">
        <preserveFixedJoint>true</preserveFixedJoint>
    </gazebo>
</robot>
